{#
  Webinar Day 0 -- theme Witty
  Ce fichier est du MJML : GrapesJsController bascule en mode MJML des qu'il
  trouve <mjml> dans le rendu (email.mjml.twig est marque deprecie dans le
  code de Mautic).

  Attention en editant : ce fichier passe par Twig. Une double accolade y
  serait interpretee comme une variable Twig. Ce theme n'en a aucune : les
  emplacements sont ecrits [ENTRE CROCHETS], a remplacer dans le builder.
  Les tokens Mautic ({contactfield=...}, {webview_url}) n'ont qu'une
  accolade et traversent Twig sans dommage.
#}
<mjml>
  <!--
  ==========================================================================
  WEBINAR TEMPLATE — DAY 0 (ANNOUNCEMENT)
  The first email that introduces the webinar. Works for any niche or topic.
  ==========================================================================

  HOW DAY 0 IS DIFFERENT FROM A REMINDER EMAIL
  A reminder email sells the DEADLINE ("last chance, tomorrow at 10:30").
  Day 0 has no deadline yet — nobody is waiting for this email. So it has to
  sell the PROBLEM instead. The reader doesn't wake up wanting a webinar;
  they wake up with a frustration. Your job here is to name that frustration
  more precisely than they could themselves, then position the webinar as
  the obvious next step.
  Practical consequences:
    • Longer body copy than a reminder. Day 0 is where you earn attention.
    • Only 2 CTAs, not 3. Pushing hard on a first touch reads as desperate.
    • No fake scarcity. Save real urgency for emails 2 and 3 — if you burn
      "last chance" on day 0, the sequence has nowhere left to go.
    • The goal is recognition, not pressure. The reader should think
      "this is literally my situation" before they see a single button.

  SIX RULES BEFORE YOU WRITE
  1. ONE goal: the click to the registration page. No links to your blog,
     socials, or homepage. Every extra link splits your click-through rate.
  2. ONE destination: both buttons point to the SAME URL. Only the wording
     changes.
  3. Write to ONE person. "You", never "you all". It should read like an
     email written for them, not broadcast at them.
  4. Sell the REGISTRATION, not the webinar. This email earns the click;
     the landing page does the selling.
  5. Benefits over topics. Not "we'll cover X", but "you'll leave able to
     do Y in Z minutes".
  6. Keep at least 60% real text. Image-heavy emails hit spam filters and
     show up blank for the ~40% of opens with images off.

  HOW TO USE THIS FILE
  - Every block has a [PLACEHOLDER] plus a WHY / HOW comment above it.
  - Never reorder the sections: they follow the reading path
    (attention → recognition → problem → cost → answer → proof → action).
  - Before sending, Ctrl+F for "[" — if anything is left, you're not done.
  ==========================================================================
  -->

  <mj-head>
    <!--
    Attributs par defaut MJML (mj-divider/mj-button) : safe a definir ici
    plutot que balise par balise, aucune des deux n'a de css-class qui
    pourrait entrer en conflit avec cette valeur par defaut.
    - mj-divider 1px (au lieu des 4px par defaut MJML — "le filet trop gros").
    - mj-button 16px (au lieu des 13px par defaut MJML — lisibilite CTA).
    NE PAS faire pareil pour mj-text : ses classes (.h1/.h2/.h3/.lead...)
    definissent chacune leur propre line-height dans le <mj-style> ci-dessous,
    et un defaut mj-attributes se compile en style inline qui gagnerait
    contre elles. Le line-height des paragraphes simples est donc pose
    directement sur chaque <mj-text> concerne, plus bas dans le corps.
    -->
    <mj-attributes>
      <mj-divider border-width="1px" border-color="#e5e5e5" />
      <mj-button font-size="16px" />
    </mj-attributes>
    <mj-style>
      @import url('https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Poppins:wght@300;400;500;600;700;800&amp;family=Inter:wght@300;400;500;600;700&amp;display=swap');
      .h1 { font-family: 'Poppins', sans-serif; font-size: 29px; font-weight: 700; line-height: 1.28; letter-spacing: -0.7px; color: #000000; }
      .h2 { font-family: 'Poppins', sans-serif; font-size: 24px; font-weight: 700; line-height: 1.3; letter-spacing: -0.5px; color: #000000; }
      .h3 { font-family: 'Poppins', sans-serif; font-size: 18px; font-weight: 600; line-height: 1.4; color: #000000; }
      .eyebrow { font-family: 'Poppins', sans-serif; font-size: 13px; font-weight: 600; letter-spacing: 1.2px; text-transform: uppercase; color: #999999; }
      .meta { font-size: 14px; font-weight: 500; color: #666666; }
      .micro { font-size: 12px; font-weight: 400; color: #999999; }
      .quote { font-style: italic; font-weight: 300; line-height: 1.7; color: #000000; }
      .strong { font-weight: 600; }
      a { color: #000000; text-decoration: underline; }
      @media only screen and (max-width:480px) {
        .h1 { font-size: 24px !important; letter-spacing: -0.4px !important; }
        .h2 { font-size: 21px !important; }
        .h3 { font-size: 16px !important; }
      }
    </mj-style>
  </mj-head>

  <mj-body width="640px" background-color="#ffffff">

    <!-- ====================================================================
    1. LOGO
    WHY: sender recognition in under half a second. A reader who identifies
    the brand before reading drops their guard. A clean logo on white also
    signals "legitimate business email" to spam filters.
    HOW: 160-200px wide, transparent PNG, hosted on your own domain (never
    Drive or Dropbox — those links break). Always fill in the alt text.
    ==================================================================== -->
    <mj-section padding="36px 24px 28px">
      <mj-column>
        <mj-image src="http://mautic.digitalrocket.pt/media/images/logo-digitalrocket.png" width="180px" alt="[Brand name]" fluid-on-mobile="true" />
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

    <mj-section>
      <mj-column>
        <mj-divider />
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

    <!-- ====================================================================
    2. HERO VISUAL (GIF or image)
    WHY: the brain reads an image far faster than a sentence. This visual is
    not decoration — it announces the SUBJECT or the FORMAT (the host's face,
    a screen from the method, a peek at what they'll build). On day 0 a face
    outperforms an abstract graphic: people register for people.
    HOW: 600px wide, 16:9 (600×338). GIF under 1 MB, JPG under 200 KB.
    Descriptive alt text is mandatory — if images are blocked, the alt still
    has to sell. Avoid text baked into the image: unreadable on mobile and
    penalised by filters.
    ==================================================================== -->
    <mj-section padding="28px 24px">
      <mj-column>
        <mj-image src="https://via.placeholder.com/600x338/000000/ffffff?text=HERO+600x338" width="600px" alt="[Describe the visual: what the reader will see or learn]" fluid-on-mobile="true" />
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

    <!-- ====================================================================
    3. TITLE BLOCK — THE PROMISE
    Assume 80% of readers will only read this block. It has five parts, in
    this exact order. Don't skip any of them.
    Heading hierarchy on purpose: h2 (hook) -> h1 (headline) -> h3 (subhead).
    All three read as real headings, not body text — that's what makes the
    block feel impactful at a glance instead of like a wall of paragraphs.
    ==================================================================== -->
    <mj-section padding="16px 24px 0px">
      <mj-column>

        <!--
        3a. HOOK LINE (day-0 specific)
        WHY: this is the line a reminder email doesn't need. It buys the two
        seconds you need to earn the headline. It works by pattern
        interruption: a truth the reader agrees with instantly, followed by
        an unexpected turn.
        HOW: two short lines separated by <br>, 4-9 words each.
          Line 1 = a statement they nod at.
          Line 2 = the twist that makes it your subject.
        Formats that work:
          • Familiar truth + reversal — "Everyone wants X. / Almost nobody does Y."
          • Insider observation      — "[Audience] love [X]. / So does [gatekeeper]."
          • Contradiction            — "You're doing everything right. / It's still not working."
        Cultural or local references land hard here — but only if you share
        that culture with the reader. Never fake it.
        -->
        <mj-text css-class="h2" align="center" padding-bottom="18px">
          [HOOK — 2 short lines. Line 1: something they instantly agree with.<br />Line 2: the twist that opens your topic.]
        </mj-text>

        <!--
        3b. HEADLINE
        WHY: it names the transformation. Day 0 has no deadline to lean on,
        so the headline has to carry the value on its own.
        HOW: 6 to 14 words. Pick ONE angle:
          • Outcome  — "[Result] in [timeframe], without [pain]"
          • Gap      — "What [audience] gets wrong about [topic] in [year]"
          • Method   — "[Topic A], [B] & [C]: the method that [outcome]"
        Be specific. A number always beats an adjective, and a concrete noun
        always beats a category.
        BANNED: "Join our webinar", "Newsletter #12", "Don't miss out" —
        zero tension, zero clicks.
        -->
        <mj-text css-class="h1" align="center" padding-bottom="16px">
          [HEADLINE — 6 to 14 words naming the transformation. Ex. "[Topic]: how to [outcome] in [year]"]
        </mj-text>

        <!--
        3c. SUBHEADLINE — THE QUALIFIER
        WHY: the headline makes the claim, the subheadline makes it credible
        and filters the audience. It answers "what do I actually walk away
        with?".
        HOW: one line, 6-14 words. Add the measurable part the headline
        couldn't fit: "that actually generate qualified leads in [year]",
        "even if you're a team of one".
        -->
        <mj-text css-class="h3" align="center" padding-bottom="14px">
          [SUBHEADLINE — 1 line, the concrete measurable result. Ex. "That actually turn into qualified leads"]
        </mj-text>

        <!--
        3d. SOCIAL PROOF
        WHY: conformity bias. "Already taken by 2,600 marketers" removes
        perceived risk faster than any argument you could write. On day 0
        it's also your only credibility signal — the reader has no reason to
        trust you yet.
        HOW: one REAL number, in this order of strength:
        past attendees > clients served > average rating > a recognisable
        client name. No number yet? Use experience ("12 years doing [X]")
        or delete the block. Never invent one.
        -->
        <mj-text css-class="meta" align="center" padding-bottom="18px">
          [SOCIAL PROOF — one verifiable number. Ex. "Already attended by 2,600+ [audience]"]
        </mj-text>

        <!--
        3e. COST OF ENTRY
        WHY: the reader is unconsciously pricing this in time. Stating
        "1 min read" removes that objection before it forms.
        HOW: be honest — roughly 200 words per minute. Day 0 runs longer than
        a reminder, so 1-2 min is normal. You can also stack it:
        "(1 min read — 45 min live)".
        -->
        <mj-text css-class="micro" align="center">
          [COST OF ENTRY — ex. "(reading time: 1 min)"]
        </mj-text>
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

    <!-- ====================================================================
    4. CTA #1 — FOR READERS ALREADY SOLD
    WHY: roughly 15% of readers are ready the moment they've read the
    promise. Forcing them through 400 more words loses them. This early
    button captures them at no cost to everyone else.
    HOW: the line above states WHEN — on day 0 this is information, not
    pressure, so keep it factual (day, date, time). The button label is
    written in the FIRST PERSON ("Save my seat"): the reader reads their own
    commitment, which measurably lifts clicks. Action verb + benefit. Never
    "Click here" or "Learn more" — no promise, no click.
    ==================================================================== -->
    <mj-section padding="36px 24px 0px">
      <mj-column>
        <mj-text align="center" font-family="'Poppins', sans-serif" font-size="17px" font-weight="700" color="#000000" padding-bottom="18px">
          [DATE LINE — ex. "🟢 WEBINAR | Thursday, [date] at [time]"]
        </mj-text>
        <mj-button href="[REGISTRATION_URL]">
          [CTA 1 — 2 to 5 words, first person. Ex. "Save my seat"]
        </mj-button>
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

    <mj-section padding="36px 24px 0px">
      <mj-column>
        <mj-divider />
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

    <!-- ====================================================================
    5. BODY — THE P.A.S. STRUCTURE
    Problem → Agitation → Solution. Name the pain before offering the cure.
    A reader who recognises themselves in your description of the problem
    grants you authority on the solution — this is the single highest-
    leverage part of a day-0 email.
    RULE: one idea per paragraph, two lines maximum. Dense blocks kill
    mobile reading. White space isn't emptiness, it's pacing.
    ==================================================================== -->
    <mj-section padding="36px 24px 0px">
      <mj-column>

        <!--
        5a. PERSONALISED GREETING
        WHY: the cocktail-party effect — a first name grabs attention
        involuntarily. Typically worth 15-25% more engagement.
        HOW: ALWAYS use a fallback with the pipe, or contacts with no first
        name get "Hi ,". Mautic syntax: {contactfield=firstname|there}
        Also: no space before the comma. It's a small thing that instantly
        reads as automated.
        -->
        <mj-text font-weight="400" line-height="1.6" padding-bottom="20px">
          Hi <span class="atwho-inserted" data-fr-verified="true">{contactfield=firstname|there}</span>,
        </mj-text>

        <!--
        5b. THE SITUATION — where the reader lives
        WHY: open in their world, not yours. The first sentence has one job:
        make them think "that's me". Describe the constraint they operate
        under, not the topic you want to teach.
        HOW: 1-2 short paragraphs. Name the specific circumstance — the small
        team, the missing budget, the ten hats they wear. Specificity is what
        makes it feel personal at scale.
        BANNED: "We're excited to invite you to…" — that's about you, and
        that's where they stop reading.
        -->
        <mj-text line-height="1.6" padding-bottom="14px">
          [SITUATION — 1 sentence describing the reader's constraint. Ex. "When you're the only one handling [X], [topic] turns into a puzzle."]
        </mj-text>
        <mj-text line-height="1.6" padding-bottom="20px">
          [WHY NOW — 1 sentence on what changed recently and made it worse. Ex. "Especially since [change] took over in [year]."]
        </mj-text>

        <!--
        5c. THE PROBLEM — named out loud
        WHY: this sentence gives them permission to be confused. Telling them
        the confusion is widespread and justified removes shame, and a reader
        who isn't defensive keeps reading.
        HOW: one sentence, slightly heavier weight so it stands out when
        scanned. Add the absolving clause — "and for good reason", "it's not
        their fault".
        -->
        <mj-text font-weight="400" line-height="1.6" padding-bottom="20px">
          [PROBLEM — 1 sentence. Ex. "The truth? Most [audience] are lost on this — and for good reason."]
        </mj-text>

        <!--
        5d. VERBATIM QUOTES — the identification device
        WHY: this is the most powerful block in a day-0 email and the one
        most people skip. Quoting your audience's exact words does something
        no polished copy can: it proves you've had this conversation before.
        The reader recognises their own internal monologue, and recognition
        converts better than persuasion.
        HOW: 2 quotes, 1-2 lines each. Pull them from real sources — sales
        calls, support tickets, DMs, discovery questionnaires, comments.
        Keep the messy, spoken phrasing, including the hesitations. Do NOT
        write them yourself in clean marketing English; it's obvious and it
        destroys the effect.
          Quote 1 → the confusion ("everyone talks about X, but what do I
                    actually DO?")
          Quote 2 → the paralysis ("I don't know what to prioritise anymore")
        -->
        <mj-text css-class="quote" padding-bottom="20px">
          <p style="margin:0 0 10px 0;">💬 "[QUOTE 1 — their real words about the confusion. Keep it messy and spoken.]"</p>
          <p style="margin:0;">💬 "[QUOTE 2 — their real words about not knowing what to prioritise.]"</p>
        </mj-text>

        <!--
        5e. AGITATION — the cost of doing nothing
        WHY: loss aversion is about twice as motivating as the prospect of
        gain. Naming what inaction costs is what turns a nodding reader into
        a clicking one.
        HOW: two short paragraphs. First the immediate consequence (guessing,
        wasted effort, no way to tell if it's working). Then the slow one —
        the thing that quietly gets deprioritised, month after month, until
        it's a real gap. Stay factual: exaggerated fear reads as manipulation
        and triggers the unsubscribe, not the click.
        -->
        <mj-text font-weight="400" line-height="1.6" padding-bottom="12px">
          [IMMEDIATE COST — 1 sentence. Ex. "So you keep moving in the dark, with no real way to know if any of it works."]
        </mj-text>
        <mj-text font-weight="400" line-height="1.6" padding-bottom="20px">
          [SLOW COST — 1 sentence on what keeps getting pushed back behind other priorities. 🫣]
        </mj-text>

        <!--
        5f. THE PIVOT
        WHY: one short line that hands the reader the exit. After the
        negative stretch, it reframes you as the person who already solved
        this. Short is the point — momentum matters more than eloquence.
        HOW: max 8 words. "That's exactly what we help you fix."
        -->
        <mj-text line-height="1.6">
          [PIVOT — 1 short line. Ex. "That's exactly what we help you fix."]
        </mj-text>
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

    <mj-section padding="36px 24px 0px">
      <mj-column>
        <mj-divider />
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

    <!-- ====================================================================
    6. WEBINAR DETAILS — THE PROOF IT'S REAL
    WHY: the body sold the problem; this block proves the answer exists and
    is concrete. It's also the block scanners jump to, so it has to work on
    its own: label, name, date, topic, agenda. Anyone landing here cold
    should understand what they're signing up for in five seconds.
    ==================================================================== -->
    <mj-section padding="36px 24px 0px">
      <mj-column>

        <!--
        6a. EYEBROW LABEL
        WHY: a structural signpost. It tells the eye "the practical
        information starts here" — that's the whole job. Keep it plain.
        -->
        <mj-text css-class="eyebrow" padding-bottom="8px">
          Webinar details
        </mj-text>

        <!--
        6b. SESSION NAME
        WHY: naming it makes it an event rather than a link. "Webinar #1"
        also implies a series, which frames you as someone who does this
        regularly — a small but real credibility cue on a first touch.
        HOW: keep it short and repeat the exact same name on the landing
        page. A mismatch between email and LP causes drop-off at the worst
        possible moment.
        -->
        <mj-text css-class="h2" padding-bottom="8px">
          [SESSION NAME — ex. "🤖 Webinar #1"]
        </mj-text>

        <!--
        6c. DATE & TIME
        WHY: pure logistics, and the #1 reason people don't register is
        "I don't know if I'm free".
        HOW: day + date + time. Add the time zone if your list spans more
        than one. Re-check this EVERY time you reuse the template — a wrong
        date is the fastest way to lose trust.
        -->
        <mj-text css-class="meta" padding-bottom="24px">
          [DAY, DATE at TIME + time zone if needed]
        </mj-text>

        <!--
        6d. TOPIC LINE
        WHY: the one-sentence version of the whole email, for the person who
        scrolled straight here. It should be able to stand completely alone.
        HOW: "Topic:" in bold + the transformation, not the subject matter.
        -->
        <mj-text font-weight="600" line-height="1.7" padding-bottom="24px">
          <strong>Topic:</strong> [The transformation in one line — ex. "[method] that [outcome] in [year]"]
        </mj-text>

        <!--
        6e. AGENDA — three bullets, no more
        WHY: three is not decorative. Two feels thin, five or more dilutes
        and stops being memorable. Each bullet should kill an objection or
        promise an outcome — never announce a topic.
        HOW: recommended order, weakest to most desirable:
          Bullet 1 → the ingredients / what a working version looks like
          Bullet 2 → the speed or effort objection removed ("in under an
                     hour, without sacrificing quality")
          Bullet 3 → your signature method, named. Best one last: it's the
                     line they remember when they decide.
        Bold the 3-5 words that carry the value in each bullet — most people
        read bullets by their bold parts only.
        ❌ "Introduction to the topic"  →  ✅ "Why 80% of [X] stall out"
        -->
        <mj-text font-family="'Poppins', sans-serif" font-size="14px" font-weight="600" padding-bottom="14px">
          📌 Agenda:
        </mj-text>
        <mj-text line-height="1.85">
          <ul style="margin:0 0 0 18px;padding:0;list-style:none;">
            <li style="margin-bottom:12px;">[BULLET 1 — the ingredients of <span class="strong">a method that actually works in [year]</span>]</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom:12px;">[BULLET 2 — the effort objection removed: <span class="strong">"[outcome] in under [time]"</span>, without [trade-off]]</li>
            <li>[BULLET 3 — <span class="strong">your named signature method</span>, the most desirable one, last]</li>
          </ul>
        </mj-text>
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

    <!-- ====================================================================
    7. CTA #2 — THE CLOSE
    WHY: peak intent. They've just read exactly what they get. Don't make
    them scroll back up to find a button.
    HOW: same URL, slightly different wording from CTA 1 — variation avoids
    the copy-paste feel and lets you see which phrasing pulls. On day 0,
    stop here: two CTAs is right for a first touch. Add the third one in the
    reminder emails, where real urgency justifies it.
    ==================================================================== -->
    <mj-section padding="32px 24px 40px">
      <mj-column>
        <mj-button href="[REGISTRATION_URL]">
          [CTA 2 — variation on CTA 1. Ex. "Save my spot now 🔥"]
        </mj-button>
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

    <mj-section>
      <mj-column>
        <mj-divider />
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

    <!-- ====================================================================
    8. FOOTER — LEGAL AND DELIVERABILITY
    WHY: the unsubscribe link is legally required (GDPR / CAN-SPAM) and
    actively monitored by Gmail and Outlook. An easy unsubscribe is far
    better than a spam complaint: one complaint damages your sending
    reputation roughly a hundred times more than one opt-out.
    The postal address line was removed on purpose (account owner's call).
    Note for future editors: CAN-SPAM (US) and several other jurisdictions
    still expect a physical postal address in every commercial email — if
    this list ever sends to those regions, put it back rather than assume
    its absence was an oversight.
    HOW: never touch the {webview_url} and {unsubscribe_url} tokens. Keep
    this block quiet — small and grey — but never hidden.
    ==================================================================== -->
    <mj-section padding="32px 24px 40px">
      <mj-column>
        <mj-text align="center" font-family="'Poppins', sans-serif" font-size="15px" font-weight="600" padding-bottom="14px">
          [BRAND NAME]
        </mj-text>
        <mj-text align="center" css-class="micro" padding-bottom="14px">
          This email was sent to <span class="atwho-inserted" data-fr-verified="true">{contactfield=email}</span>
        </mj-text>
        <mj-text align="center" font-size="12px">
          <a href="{webview_url}" style="color:#000000;text-decoration:underline;">View in browser</a> &nbsp;|&nbsp;
          <a href="{unsubscribe_url}" style="color:#000000;text-decoration:underline;">Unsubscribe</a>
        </mj-text>
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

    <!--
    ==========================================================================
    PRE-SEND CHECKLIST (this block is a comment — leave it or delete it)
    □ Both buttons point to the same URL, with UTM parameters.
    □ Day, date and time match across subject, body and landing page.
    □ Every [BRACKET] replaced — run Ctrl+F on "[".
    □ The two quotes are REAL quotes from real people, not invented.
    □ {contactfield=firstname|there} tested on a contact with no first name.
    □ Test send opened on mobile with images off: still readable, CTA still
      visible.
    □ Alt text filled in on every image.
    □ Subject line under 45 characters, preheader extends it.
    □ No urgency language you'll need again in the reminder emails.
    ==========================================================================
    -->

  </mj-body>
</mjml>
